1. Tasks Performed

This period reflects the significantly greater productivity that
typically accompanies the summer months. We have made considerable
progress in particular on software architecture for modular
distributable tools, notably a demonstration of an integration with
WELD (Web-Based Electronic Design), a Ptolemy/Java integration
demonstration, and major headway on the Tcl/Java front.  External
interactions were also particularly intensive.


2. Significant Accomplishments

2.1 Tcl/Java Interface

John Reekie has written an interface between Tcl and Java that uses
the Java Reflect class.  This interface is considerably easier to use
than Sun's TclJava0.4 interface, making the integration more transparent.
However, measurements indicate that the interface is 2-3 times slower.
Fortunately, discussions with Sun (at the Tcl/Tk conference in Boston)
indicate that Sun is seriously committed to improving their interface.

John and Christopher Hylands have been using this experience to design
the next generation Tcl/Java interface, which will use the JNI (Java
Native Interface). They decided to work with Tcl8 so that when Itcl3.0
comes out, we can just drop in the interface. One of the key problems
in such an interface is that Tcl has no types, while Java is strongly
typed. A consequence is that method names are not always sufficient in
Tcl for identifying a corresponding Java method. The types of the
arguments may also need to be known. For a first cut solution, they are
planning on requiring the user to have the type signature in the method
call. The calls would look like:

::java::new foo .foo
.foo bar {int int object} 4 myobject

for a Java class foo that has a .bar method that takes an int and an
object and returns an int.  An alternative that is not as readable
but would be more efficient would look like:

.foo bar (IL/pt/kernel/Foo;)I 4 myobject

This uses the standard encoding from the Java virtual machine.


2.2 Tycho/WELD Integration

Collaboration with the WELD project (Web-Based Electronic Design),
headed by Richard Newton, continues. John Reekie has built into Tycho a
number of features to support access to WELD databases and
applications. The intent of the integration is to use Tycho to provide
more complex user interfaces to WELD design tools that are operated
over the network. Tycho provides a significantly higher-level platform
for user interface design than Java, on which WELD is based.

John Reekie has constructed a demo using Ptolemy remotely via the WELD
protocol with Tycho providing the user interface.  He gave the demo
at DAC (the Design Automation Conference).  We have learned from
this that some refinement of the WELD protocol is needed to support
more dynamic simulations. Using the WELD protocol would have a number
of advantages like independence from the user interface, ability to
monitor just the data of interest from various simulations, perhaps a
workflow application that supports dynamic monitoring, and control of
any simulation.

This work has contributed some infrastructure to Tycho, including
persistent object support classes, which Tycho uses to access the WELD
database (written in Java), a client-side interface to a WELD server
(Java), and a simple server process that runs Ptolemy under the control
of Tycho. This is perhaps the first client-server implementation of
Ptolemy.

To make Tycho "WELD-aware", John Reekie wrote a subclass of the
Resource class, called ResourceWELD, and added the protocol name "weld"
to the existing list ("http", "mailto", "file"). Because of this
approach, access to the WELD server is transparent: if you open a file
browser (in tycho -java) and enter weld://machinename, you will get a
listing of the contents of the WELD server on that machine. John has
installed on the main WELD server machine (called
"yoyodyne.eecs.berkeley.edu") a graph editor with a simple dataflow
graph. There is a menu entry on the graph editor called "Run Remote
Ptolemy", which will generate a set of textual commands to Ptolemy and
send them to a Tycho server. The Tycho server can also be run from the
WELD workflow applet.

How it works: The ResourceWELD class creates a single instance of the
Java class weld.persistent.TclWeldDirectory. This class, which uses the
Tycho-Java interface, creates and manipulates various kinds of Java
objects provided by the WELD group, which know how to access the
database running on their server. The database is completely general,
but they have built DirObject and DataObject classes to allow clients
to build a simple file-system-like structure on it. The
TclWeldDirectory class makes some effort to cache information about the
database locally, as Tycho tends to make a lot of calls to check for
file existence, whether it's a directory, and so on.  This is similar
to the caching of http accesses that Tycho does.

To execute commands in a remote Ptolemy, the graph editor creates an
instance of the Java object weld.client.TclClient. This class is
basically just a Tcl-interface wrapper around one of the Weld Java
classes, which implements their server protocol. The editor generates
the textual command that describe the the graph, and gives it to its
TclClient Java object, which in turn creates a WELD CS_Client object
that transmits the graph description to the Tycho "server". The server
is a C++ program waiting on a socket that places the data into a file,
and execs a script, which in turn execs tycho -ptiny (which runs
ptolemy with textual interface). When this is done, the server takes
the data from the output file generated by Ptolemy (see below) and
returns it to the client object; eventually, a Tycho editor pops up
with the returned data in it.

Lessons learned: First, running Ptolemy in client server mode is pretty
easy because of its Tcl-based textual command interface. Second,
security is a big problem with this setup, since the server is
executing an arbitrary Tcl script. This requires further work. Third,
most Ptolemy applications need to be modified to work this way -- to
produce the output file. Fourth, the relative ease with which John was
able to get Tycho talking to WELD indicates that we're on the right
track with our Tycho-java interface. Finally, this is interesting but a
lot more development would be needed to fully realize interactive
executions, as opposed to executions that put their results in a file.
The WELD people seem interested in talking about extending their
protocol to better support Ptolemy-style interactive simulations.


2.3 Java Performance

Dick Stevens (on sabbatical in our group from the Naval Research Labs)
designed an experiment to monitor the details of Java garbage collection.
The results indicate that current implementations of Java rely very
heavily on garbage collection, including the management of automatic
variables and other temporaries that would normally be managed on a stack.
In view of this, there seems to be little point in trying to design code
to minimize garbage collection.  Instead, performance will depend on the
quality and granularity of the garbage collection itself.


2.4 Software Improvements

John Reekie improved the menu mechanism in Tycho to identify menu entries
with tags rather than by their label.  This allows more flexibility in
dynamically modifying menus.

Tom Lane (of Structured Software Systems) completed a major redesign of
the higher-order functions (HOF) mechanism in Ptolemy.  This mechanism is
central the ability to specify scalable applications compactly, and may
turn out to be central to supporting mutable systems in the future.
The previous mechanism was full of bugs concerning its interaction with
hierarchy and with the notion of delays on arcs.

John Reekie added a simple animation facility to the Slate, one that
supports linear moving and reshaping of graphical items. The idea is to
use this infrastructure in design visualization. An animation is driven
by an instance of a new class called Animator; Animator is a sub-class
of Interactor, but generates interaction events by itself instead of in
response to mouse events. The animate{} method of the Slate class
provides a simple interface to objects of this class. For example, to
move an object 50 pixels south-east, execute:

  $slate animate move $id 50 50 -duration 2000

The -duration argument is the approximate time to complete the
animation, in "Tcl time." To reshape an object, execute something like: 

  $slate animate reshape $id 50 50 -aspects vertex-1

Animation is asynchronous, so multiple animation calls overlap in
effect. To synchronize a series of animation sequences, inserts calls
like this:

  $slate animate wait

