2.5 Multidimensional Signal Processing Option--Questech

The main activity during this period has been an ongoing investigation
into a dataflow model called Regular Dataflow graphs (RDFGs).
RDFGs compactly represent multidimensional algorithms with regular
control and data flow, and thus match multidimensional signal processing
applications well. We hope to use them to improve the ability of
multidimensional dataflow representations in Ptolemy to exploit
parallel signal processing hardware.

RDFGs have been studied before, although unsatisfactorily, in the context
of wavefront array processors.  RDFGs are distuinguished by their highly
regular structure; this regularity leads to descriptions that are
exponentially more compact than descriptions of normal graphs.  Hence,
very large graphs can be described using very little space, and infinite
graphs can be described using finite space (infinite graphs arise as
precedence graphs in signal processing where the time dimension is
infinite).  RDFGs can model algorithms where the structure of the
algorithm leads to highly homogenous processing elements and activities,
like matrix multiplication.  RDFGs can also be used to model the
precedence graphs of multidimensional data flow graphs (MD DFG), which is
our practical motivation for studying them. 

We have been interested in designing certain graph-theoretic algorithms
for RDFGs that arise commonly in design prototyping environments.  The
goal is to have algorithms that work on the compact description, and hence
take much less time than conventional algorithms that work on traditional
graph descriptions (like incidence matrices and adjacency lists).  Our
first results in this area are discouraging in the sense that problems
such as cycle detection and deadlock detection turn out to be NP-complete
(proven in a forthcoming technical report).  However, the NP-completeness
arises when the size of the problem increases in certain dimensions;
practically, the increase in these dimensions does not occur much.  Hence,
we have been pursuing heuristics, and the possibility of polynomial time
algorithms when the particular dimensions that cause NP-completeness are
assumed to be non-increasing (usually true in practice).  A concrete
example of such a "dimension" is the number of arcs that each node has in
an RDFG.  Usually, this is a fixed small number such as 4 or 5, and does
not increase even if the number of nodes is very large. 

Another problem of interest is the computation of the maximum cycle mean; 
this quantity determines the maximum rate at which the graph can be
executed and is thus metric used for designing and evaluating
multiprocessor schedulers.

Ultimately, having such algorithms will enable us to better analyze the
(infinite) precedence graphs that arise in MD DFGs.  This in turn, will
allow us to develop good multiprocessor schedulers for multidimensional
dataflow in Ptolemy, something that is currently not there. 

Since RDFGs are described using integer matrices, and cycles can be
described using non-negative, integral vectors in the null space of these
matrices, the theory of lattices and integer linear algebra is relevant
here.  We are engaged in deciphering the useful aspects of this theory, to
see how it can be applied in our context.  An example of integer linear
algebra is the problem of computing linear dependence or independence of a
set of integral vectors by *non-negative integral* coefficients
(contrasted with linear algebra where the coefficients can be real
numbers).  This naturally requires the use of results from the study of
Diophantine equations and geometric lattices. 


