3.2 Software Infrastructure

We are investigating a package from Bell Labs that might help maintain
security in distributed Tycho and Ptolemy applications.  Here is some
information about it:

   The first internet release of NSBD, Not-So-Bad Distribution is now
   available.

   NSBD is an open WWW-based authenticating software distribution
   system. NSBD adds two basic things to simple web downloads: it
   authenticates maintainers of packages with digital signatures so
   users can trust where the packages come from, and it supplies a
   means of checking for updates to the packages and automatically
   downloading and installing the updates. NSBD uses "Pretty Good(Tm)
   Privacy" (PGP(Tm)) for authentication. NSBD's focus is on security,
   leaving as much control as practical in the users' hands. The single
   NSBD program handles the operations done by both maintainers of
   packages and users of packages.

   NSBD is free software, released under the terms of the GNU General
   Public License. At this point it is a beta release, until enough
   time has elapsed to allow feedback from more users. Binaries are
   available for a variety of Unix platforms, and the source code is
   also available. It is written in C and TCL/TK. It is portable to
   most Unix platforms, and it shouldn't be too difficult to port it to
   PC-Windows (any volunteers to help?).

   The home page of NSBD is
       http://www.bell-labs.com/nsbd


